---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
  title:
    "I Asked Gemini 3.1 Pro to Audit My Website's Performance — While Looking at a Live Dashboard",
  description:
    "A first-person exploration of Gemini 3.1 Pro's multimodal capabilities: navigating a live Looker Studio dashboard, reading interactive charts, and verifying performance hypotheses against the actual website code.",
  pubDate: "2026-02-22",
  tags: ["AI", "Web Performance", "Gemini 3.1", "Looker Studio"],
};

const currentLang = "en";
const alternateHref = "/blog/gemini-pagespeed-audit/";

const imgBase = "/assets/imgs/blog/gemini-pagespeed";
---

<MainLayout
  title="I Asked Gemini 3.1 Pro to Audit My Website Performance | Alexis Gomel"
  description="A first-person exploration of Gemini 3.1 Pro's multimodal capabilities: navigating a live Looker Studio dashboard, reading interactive charts, and verifying performance hypotheses against the actual website code."
  currentLang={currentLang}
  alternateHref={alternateHref}
>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/cv.css" slot="head" />
  <style is:inline>
    .blog-wrap {
      max-width: 760px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 120px 24px 80px;
      line-height: 1.8;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      color: #333;
    }
    .blog-wrap h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.2rem);
      font-weight: 700;
      line-height: 1.25;
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }
    .blog-meta {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: #888;
      margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    }
    .blog-wrap h2 {
      font-size: 1.35rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin-top: 2.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
      border-bottom: 2px solid #f0f0f0;
      padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
    }
    .blog-wrap p {
      margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    }
    .blog-img {
      width: 100%;
      border-radius: 8px;
      border: 1px solid #e5e5e5;
      box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.07);
      margin: 1rem 0 0.4rem;
    }
    .img-caption {
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      color: #999;
      text-align: center;
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }
    .img-pair {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 12px;
      margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    }
    @media (max-width: 600px) {
      .img-pair {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
    }
    .tag {
      display: inline-block;
      background: #f4f4f4;
      border-radius: 4px;
      padding: 2px 10px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: #555;
      margin-right: 6px;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }
    .highlight-box {
      background: #f8f8f8;
      border-left: 4px solid var(--primary, #e74c3c);
      padding: 14px 18px;
      border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
      font-size: 0.97rem;
    }
    /* Scrollable audit embed */
    .audit-scroll {
      border: 1px solid #e0e0e0;
      border-radius: 10px;
      max-height: 520px;
      overflow-y: auto;
      padding: 28px 32px;
      background: #fafafa;
      font-size: 0.93rem;
      line-height: 1.75;
      margin: 1.5rem 0;
    }
    .audit-scroll h3 {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin-top: 1.5rem;
    }
    .audit-scroll h4 {
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin-top: 1.2rem;
    }
    .audit-scroll table {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      margin: 1rem 0;
    }
    .audit-scroll th,
    .audit-scroll td {
      border: 1px solid #ddd;
      padding: 6px 10px;
      text-align: left;
    }
    .audit-scroll th {
      background: #f0f0f0;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .audit-scroll tr:nth-child(even) td {
      background: #f7f7f7;
    }
    .badge-confirmed {
      color: #27ae60;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .badge-refuted {
      color: #e74c3c;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .verdict {
      display: inline-block;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      border-radius: 4px;
      padding: 2px 8px;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .verdict.confirmed {
      background: #eafaf1;
      color: #1e8449;
    }
    .verdict.refuted {
      background: #fdedec;
      color: #c0392b;
    }
    .verdict.partial {
      background: #fef9e7;
      color: #9a7d0a;
    }
    .scroll-hint {
      text-align: center;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      color: #aaa;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }
  </style>

  <article class="blog-wrap">
    <span class="tag">AI</span>
    <span class="tag">Web Performance</span>
    <span class="tag">Gemini 3.1</span>
    <span class="tag">Looker Studio</span>

    <h1>Can AI build my website, and audit it too?</h1>
    <h2>I asked the new Gemini 3.1 Pro to step in as my website's analyst.</h2>

    <p class="blog-meta">Alexis Gomel · 22 February 2026</p>

    <p>
      Last Thursday, Google released Gemini 3.1 Pro through Antigravity. I was
      curious about one specific capability: could it actually <em>see</em> and <em
        >reason</em
      > about data in a live, interactive web report like a human analyst would.
    </p>
    <p>So I ran a small experiment.</p>

    <p>
      Over the last year, i've been developing my website with the help of AI.
      This means all my website's code is available for the AI agents, and the
      documentation related to it's design choices.
    </p>

    <p>
      Along side this development, I also have dashboards to track the analytics
      of my website. In particular I have a dashboard that displays a daily
      PageSpeed audit to check for performance changes and improvement
      oportunities.
    </p>

    <p>
      Usually, this means i'd have a look at the data and then report to the
      agent to brainstorm and imeplement improvements for my website. So, this
      is a small experiment to test what happens when the AI knows my codebase,
      and a can also audit the performance and suggest changes, as well as a
      test on the multimodal capabilities of the new models. Can it understand
      and interact with my dashboard like a normal user, to find the relevant
      data?.
    </p>

    <p>
      The expetiment consist of the following: - Navigate the dashboard to find
      the performance metrics. - Make an audit report identifying possible
      improvements based on the metrics, while taking into account the content
      of my website. - Perform the proposed imrpovements and check if the
      changes has the expected results.
    </p>

    <h2>The Setup</h2>
    <p>
      I built a custom analytics pipeline for this site using GA4, BigQuery, and
      Looker Studio. Part of it is a daily PageSpeed audit: a threaded Python
      Cloud Function reads my sitemap, calls the
      <a
        href="https://developers.google.com/speed/docs/insights/v5/get-started"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">PageSpeed Insights API</a
      >
      7 times per page, and stores the results in BigQuery. Because performance scores
      naturally vary between runs, the dashboard shows the <strong
        >median of those 7 measurements</strong
      > to get a stable reading. Only the Performance metric has a wide spread; the
      others (SEO, Accessibility, Best Practices) are essentially deterministic.
    </p>
    <div class="highlight-box">
      You can explore the live dashboard yourself at
      <a href="/projects/webanalytics/"
        >alexisgomel.com/projects/webanalytics/</a
      >.
    </div>
    <p>
      I asked a Gemini 3.1 Pro agent with access to my website's codebase to
      navigate to the PageSpeed section of that report, read the charts, and
      produce a technical audit; without me interpreting anything for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 1: Navigating the Dashboard</h2>
    <p>
      The first challenge for it's multimodal capabilities. I gave it access to
      my personal dashboard, which meant using the browser, and undestanding
      visual layout and context.
    </p>

    <p>
      It opened the Looker Studio report and correctly identified the "Page
      speed" tab, then the "Current state" sub-section.
    </p>
    <div class="img-pair">
      <div>
        <img
          src={`${imgBase}/02_pagespeed_menu.png`}
          alt="Gemini navigating to the Page speed menu"
          class="blog-img"
        />
        <p class="img-caption">
          Correctly finding the Page speed → Current state section
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <img
          src={`${imgBase}/03_pagespeed_current_state.png`}
          alt="Page speed current state dashboard"
          class="blog-img"
        />
        <p class="img-caption">
          The PageSpeed dashboard with Best Practices, SEO and Performance
          charts
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Step 2: The Date Incident</h2>
    <p>
      A fist hickup. The dashboard has a date range filter, and without being
      asked, Gemini opened it and started changing it. It typed "028" into the
      offset field, accidentally pulling the filter back 28 days instead of
      staying on today's data. This was a surprise, as it was not requiered to
      check the current status of the website, and a small telltale of things to
      come.
    </p>
    <div class="img-pair">
      <div>
        <img
          src={`${imgBase}/05_date_picker_mishap.png`}
          alt="Gemini mistyping 028 in date filter"
          class="blog-img"
        />
        <p class="img-caption">
          The model proactively opened the date filter and typed "028" —
          expanding scope without being asked.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <img
          src={`${imgBase}/06_date_applied.png`}
          alt="Date filter correctly set to today"
          class="blog-img"
        />
        <p class="img-caption">
          After my correction: filter set cleanly to 22 Feb 2026 – 22 Feb 2026
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      I corrected it with a short follow-up message and it applied the right
      date immediately. But this is something I've noticed comparing Gemini to
      Claude: Gemini tends to <strong
        >expand the scope of a task without checking in first</strong
      >. While in my experience, Claude is more likely to ask for confirmation
      before acting outside the scope of the task. Neither approach is
      universally better, but for tasks involving live data or production
      systems, it's worth keeping in mind.
    </p>
    <p>
      That said, compared to Gemini 3.0, which would loop on screenshots or lose
      track of the report entirely, 3.1 Pro's navigation was decisively more
      reliable. One small correction on my part versus complete inability to
      complete the task.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 3: Reading the Scrollable Charts</h2>
    <p>
      Once the date was set, the AI scrolled through the performance charts,
      including the interactive "Performance breakdown (median)" table. This
      table lists every page path with device-specific scores for Speed Index,
      FCP, LCP, CLS, and TBT. This is a nice improvement, as it is a somewhat
      elaborate interaction involving scrolling on elemnts inside the page, and
      previous versions couldn't reliably do this.
    </p>
    <img
      src={`${imgBase}/07_performance_table.png`}
      alt="Performance breakdown table in Looker Studio"
      class="blog-img"
    />
    <p class="img-caption">
      The scrollable performance table showing per-path, per-device breakdown of
      all Core Web Vital metrics
    </p>

    <h2>The Audit Report</h2>
    <p>
      From reading the charts and table, Gemini 3.1 Pro generated the following
      technical audit. It identified the bottlenecks, scored them by severity,
      and proposed fixes based on the dashboard data.
    </p>

    <p class="scroll-hint">↕ Scroll within the box to read the full report</p>
    <div class="audit-scroll">
      <h3>Executive Summary</h3>
      <p>
        The website demonstrates excellent baseline capabilities on Desktop, but
        suffers from severe performance degradation on specific Mobile pages.
        The primary bottlenecks are <strong
          >excessive main-thread blocking (TBT)</strong
        > on pages with heavy interactive components and delayed visual rendering
        (Speed Index) for mobile users.
      </p>

      <h3>Key Performance Metrics — 22/02/2026</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr
            ><th>Page</th><th>Device</th><th>Score</th><th>SI</th><th>FCP</th
            ><th>LCP</th><th>TBT</th><th>CLS</th></tr
          >
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr
            ><td>/sac/</td><td>Desktop</td><td>100</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td
            ><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/sac/</td><td>Mobile</td><td>86</td><td>0.92</td><td>0.44</td
            ><td>0.69</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/projects/</td><td>Desktop</td><td>100</td><td>1.0</td><td
              >1.0</td
            ><td>0.99</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/projects/</td><td>Mobile</td><td>78</td><td>0.98</td><td
              >0.81</td
            ><td><strong>0.22</strong></td><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/projects/webanalytics/</td><td>Desktop</td><td>60</td><td
              ><strong>0.0</strong></td
            ><td>0.99</td><td>0.99</td><td><strong>0.0</strong></td><td>1.0</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/projects/webanalytics/</td><td>Mobile</td><td
              ><strong>56</strong></td
            ><td><strong>0.02</strong></td><td>0.76</td><td>0.94</td><td
              ><strong>0.0</strong></td
            ><td>1.0</td></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/playlists/</td><td>Desktop</td><td>60</td><td>0.02</td><td
              >1.0</td
            ><td>0.99</td><td><strong>0.0</strong></td><td>1.0</td></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/playlists/</td><td>Mobile</td><td><strong>55</strong></td><td
              ><strong>0.0</strong></td
            ><td>0.89</td><td>0.81</td><td>0.03</td><td>1.0</td></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/projects/looker-studio-…</td><td>Desktop</td><td>62</td><td
              >0.23</td
            ><td>0.98</td><td>0.98</td><td><strong>0.0</strong></td><td>1.0</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/es/ (home)</td><td>Mobile</td><td><strong>39</strong></td><td
              >0.54</td
            ><td>0.53</td><td><strong>0.03</strong></td><td>0.09</td><td>1.0</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td>/es/projects/</td><td>Mobile</td><td>70</td><td>—</td><td>—</td
            ><td><strong>0.01</strong></td><td>—</td><td>1.0</td></tr
          >
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <em
          >Note: CLS is perfect (1.0) across all pages — the layout is stable
          everywhere.</em
        >
      </p>

      <h3>Findings, by Priority</h3>

      <h4>1. TBT Critical — JavaScript Execution Wall</h4>
      <p>
        Pages embedding third-party iframes (/projects/webanalytics/,
        /playlists/, /projects/looker-studio-custom-visuals/) have a TBT score
        of 0.0. The browser's main thread is completely locked during load.
        Users cannot scroll or interact until the JS payload clears.
      </p>

      <h4>2. Speed Index — Blank Screen Syndrome</h4>
      <p>
        The Speed Index on /projects/webanalytics/ and /playlists/ is near 0.0,
        meaning the page renders almost nothing visible for a long window during
        load. There are no skeleton screens or progressive reveal.
      </p>

      <h4>3. Mobile LCP — /projects/ Listing Page</h4>
      <p>
        /projects/ is a perfect 100 on desktop. On mobile, LCP drops to 0.22.
        The hero section contains autoplay videos and large unoptimized images
        served at desktop resolution.
      </p>

      <h4>4. Spanish Localization (/es/) — Worst Mobile Scores</h4>
      <p>
        Scrolling through the chart reveals the /es/ paths show catastrophically
        low mobile LCP (0.01–0.03) and the worst overall performance scores.
        Hypothesis: a render-blocking payload specific to the localization
        layer.
      </p>

      <h3>Recommendations</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Tier 1 — TBT:</strong> Add lazy loading to iframes. Use IntersectionObserver
        to defer initialization until the embed is in view. Consider a Facade pattern
        (show a static image, load iframe on click).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tier 2 — Speed Index:</strong> Add skeleton screens for heavy sections.
        Inline critical CSS.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tier 3 — Mobile LCP:</strong> Add <code>srcset</code> and <code
          >sizes</code
        > to hero images. Preload video poster frames with <code
          >fetchpriority="high"</code
        >.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Step 4: Verifying the Hypotheses in Code</h2>
    <p>
      Next I asked it to check whether its guesses were actually correct by
      scanning the website's source code. This is where the loop closed nicely.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      <strong>TBT hypothesis:</strong>
      <span class="verdict confirmed">✅ Confirmed</span><br />
      The <code>webanalytics.astro</code> file loads the Looker Studio iframe immediately
      with no
      <code>loading="lazy"</code>. The playlists page does the same for the
      primary Spotify embed.
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      <strong>Speed Index hypothesis:</strong>
      <span class="verdict confirmed">✅ Confirmed</span><br />
      There's a loading spinner in the markup, but the iframe request fires immediately
      underneath it, clogging the network before the spinner can even render cleanly.
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      <strong>Mobile LCP on /projects/:</strong>
      <span class="verdict partial">⚠ Confirmed (partially)</span><br />
      Hero images use <code>fetchpriority="high"</code> but no <code
        >srcset</code
      >. Desktop-resolution assets served to mobile. The autoplay video poster
      is also unpreloaded.
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
      <strong>Spanish localization hypothesis:</strong>
      <span class="verdict refuted">❌ Refuted</span><br />
      The <code>/es/</code> pages are physically separate Astro files with identical
      structure to the English versions, no extra JS, no localization framework running
      client-side. The extreme LCP scores on those pages are likely measurement noise
      from the specific crawl, not a code defect.
    </div>

    <p>
      Three out of four hypotheses confirmed. The fourth one was correctly
      falsified, which is actually the more interesting result. The AI looked at
      real data, generated a plausible but wrong explanation, and then corrected
      it when the code didn't support it.
    </p>

    <h2>Fixes Applied</h2>
    <p>After the verification, I had it implement the non-disruptive fixes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>loading="lazy"</code> added to the Looker Studio iframe on both the
        English and Spanish web analytics pages
      </li>
      <li>
        Responsive <code>widths</code> and <code>sizes</code> attributes added to
        the hero images on the projects listing pages
      </li>
      <li>
        Video poster preloaded via <code>&lt;link rel="preload"&gt;</code> in the
        document head
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em
        ><strong>Update (Feb 23):</strong> I checked the dashboard today to see the
        impact of these changes. Surprisingly, the TBT on the webanalytics page was
        still 0.0. Why? Because <code>loading="lazy"</code> on an iframe that sits
        "above the fold" doesn't actually stop Lighthouse from eagerly executing it.
        The browser sees it's in the initial viewport and downloads it anyway. It
        turns out a possible the true fix will require a Facade pattern (rendering
        a static image until clicked), which Gemini actually suggested as its "Tier
        1" recommendation in the audit. While this is true, it would break the user
        experience of the website. This is a good example where AI still cannot make
        a good call because it will optimize without taking into account the broader
        context of the dessign.
      </em>
    </p>

    <h2>Follow up explorations</h2>

    <p>
      The Looker Studio dashboard shows the <em>median</em> of 7 daily test runs per
      page. But to really understand what's happening, we need to look at the full
      distribution, so I aske the AI to ran a Python script against the raw data in
      BigQuery to plot the actual spread of the Performance Score and TBT metrics
      for both days.
    </p>

    <div class="img-pair">
      <div>
        <img
          src={`${imgBase}/performance_score_distribution_22.png`}
          alt="Performance score distributions on Feb 22"
          class="blog-img"
        />
        <p class="img-caption">
          Feb 22: You can clearly see the massive variation in scores on the
          mobile <code>/projects/webanalytics/</code> page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <img
          src={`${imgBase}/performance_score_distribution_23.png`}
          alt="Performance score distributions on Feb 23"
          class="blog-img"
        />
        <p class="img-caption">
          Feb 23: After the "fix", the variance on the heavy pages remains
          largely identical.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Looking at the TBT specifically, the boxplots show exactly why the native
      lazy loading attempt failed to move the needle: the main thread isn't just
      blocked, it's blocked <em>consistently</em> for thousands of milliseconds across
      every single run, cementing the need for a true Facade approach.
    </p>

    <p>
      The interesting take away from this, beyond the failed fix, is the
      potential of having a workflow where the AI can be mindful of the
      codebase, but also have access to audit and analytics data to run it's own
      experiments and test ideas and improvements like a real analyst would.
    </p>

    <div class="img-pair">
      <div>
        <img
          src={`${imgBase}/tbt_value_distribution_22.png`}
          alt="TBT distributions on Feb 22"
          class="blog-img"
        />
        <p class="img-caption">
          Feb 22: Unacceptable TBT spread on the heavy iframe pages.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <img
          src={`${imgBase}/tbt_value_distribution_23.png`}
          alt="TBT distributions on Feb 23"
          class="blog-img"
        />
        <p class="img-caption">
          Feb 23: Native <code>loading="lazy"</code> on above-the-fold content provides
          zero relief.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Reflections</h2>

    <h3>Results form the audit</h3>

    <p>
      The distribution plots revealed three things that the median scores
      masked:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The Astro foundation is rock solid:</strong> The lightweight pages
        (like <code>/sac/</code> or this very blog post) clustered tightly around
        90-100. The performance bleed is 100% isolated to the third-party embeds.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The "/es/ ghost" was just noise:</strong> Yesterday's Looker Studio
        audit flagged the Spanish home page for a catastrophic mobile score of 39.
        The distribution on Feb 22 showed massive, sloppy variance. But on Feb 23?
        That variance vanished, and the score clustered tightly at 85. This validated
        our code-check: there was no heavy localization framework ruinous to performance.
        We were chasing a ghost born from network jitter during a synthetic crawl.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The variance simply shifted:</strong> On the 22nd, <code
          >/sevilla/</code
        >
        was the unstable outlier. On the 23rd, it stabilized and <code
          >/playlists/</code
        >
        became wildly unpredictable.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The ultimate takeaway? Hunting performance bugs using a single day's
      median score can be misleading. The dashboard alerted me to the problem,
      but only by looking at the <em>distributions</em> across multiple days do you
      see the truth: the framework is stable, but third-party embeds introduce massive,
      unpredictable latency spikes that cannot be tamed by native <code
        >loading="lazy"</code
      > tags.
    </p>

    <h3>Can gemini 3.1 audit my website</h3>

    <p>
      This experiment worked better than I expected for a first try, and it
      represents a workflow I hadn't really used before: ask the AI to look at a <em
        >live</em
      >
      report, form its own interpretation, then check it against the code, and finally
      validate those assumptions against the raw data distributions.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, along the way Gemini 3.1 showed that it is eager to act rather
      than ask. For exploratory, low-stakes experiments that's mostly fine. For
      anything involving production or deployment you'd want clearer scope
      boundaries upfront. The other downside is the lack of good criteria that
      imply taking into account the broader picture. It's suggestions seem very
      reasonable and sound logic, but lack the judgement to realise that it is
      better to have a suboptimal solution but keep a better user experience.
    </p>

    <p>
      Over all the new model shows clear improvements, but have not reached the
      point where we can rely on them to make important descicions.
    </p>
